  "image_name": "2c63ae451a81acdc2191dbbfa4c7225d.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how neural circuits can be diversified at several levels beyond a simple one-input/one-output synapse. It begins with a small network in which multiple neurons are connected by chemical inhibitory synapses and gap junctions, highlighting that different inputs can interact within the same circuit.\n\nThe main diagrams focus on synapses that release both a conventional small-molecule transmitter and a peptide transmitter. A single presynaptic axon can branch to several terminals and contact different postsynaptic neurons, but the effect depends on which receptors those targets express. Some postsynaptic cells appear to receive mainly fast transmission through transmitter-gated channels, whereas others also respond to peptides through separate neuropeptide receptors.\n\nThe figure also shows that the same presynaptic input can generate different outputs depending on firing pattern: sparse versus stronger burst-like activity appears to alter transmitter release and receptor engagement. Another mechanism is axo-axonic modulation, where a second neuron synapses onto the presynaptic terminal and changes the output from one state to another, producing different activity patterns downstream. Finally, peptide signaling seems able to spread more broadly than point-to-point fast transmission, with released peptide acting at nearby sites and being limited by extracellular peptidases. Overall, the image conveys that synaptic output is context dependent, target specific, and dynamically regulated.",
